abstract: "Parasites of foundation species, such as fucoids, mussels, and barnacles,\
  \ can shape the intertidal communities that depend on them, yet their effects remain\
  \ poorly understood. On rocky shores of the Northeast Pacific, the acorn barnacle\
  \ Balanus glandula forms dense aggregations that provide habitat for other species.\
  \ We investigated infection by the microphallid trematode Maritrema gratiosum in\
  \ its second intermediate host, B. glandula, in British Columbia, Canada. First,\
  \ we described the metacercarial cysts and examined how host morphology (in spring\
  \ and summer) and reproductive output relate to infection. Second, we examined how\
  \ infection varies with host position on live mussel shells, substratum type (rock\
  \ versus mussel shells), and shore height. Third, we related prevalence and parasite\
  \ load (number of cysts per host) to local air and sea-surface temperatures. Cysts\
  \ had a mean diameter of 604 \xB1 46 \xB5m and 80% of them occurred in fewer than\
  \ 20% of hosts. Host size predicted infection differently at two scales: within\
  \ sites, larger barnacles were more likely to be infected, but among sites, infection\
  \ was most likely in populations of intermediate mean size. Among infected hosts,\
  \ parasite load was associated with host shape and became strongly size-dependent\
  \ later in the season. Brood mass scaled hyper-allometrically with somatic mass\
  \ (b = 1.27) and was unrelated to parasite load, while the scaling of parasite load\
  \ with host mass differed among sites. Infection intensity (cysts per g of somatic\
  \ tissue) did not vary with host position on mussels or between substrata, but varied\
  \ unimodally with shore height, peaking at ~2.9 m above chart datum. At a larger\
  \ spatial scale around Vancouver Island, 70.7% of barnacles were infected with site\
  \ prevalence ranging from 0 to 100%. Infection probability increased with host size\
  \ but was unrelated to temperature. Parasite load, however, increased with host\
  \ size and with warmer-than-usual sea-surface temperatures, but decreased with warmer-than-usual\
  \ air temperatures. Relationships among individual hosts did not consistently hold\
  \ across sites, seasons, or thermal environments, suggesting that host demography,\
  \ immersion time, and parasite supply jointly structure infection in this intertidal\
  \ foundation species."
